analysisWe need to parse the comment: "// @@@ If the appended element is null, the last element in the vector is also null; otherwise, the appended element is equal to the last element in the vector."

We need to write an assert statement in JML-like syntax using \old, \result, etc.

Method add(E e) returns boolean true always. The comment is about after adding element e, the last element (i.e., element at index size()-1) is either null if e is null, else equals e.

We need to use observers: we have methods: lastElement(), maybe elementAt, get, size, etc. lastElement() returns last component, but throws if empty. After add, size will increase by 1. So we can assert condition using this.lastElement(). Or we can use this.get(this.size()-1). Use \old for size before.

We need to refer to e (parameter). Condition: (e == null) => this.lastElement() == null && (! (e == null)) => this.lastElement().equals(e). Since we need equality semantics for objects, use .equals? In examples they used .equals for query(i). So we can write: assert (e == null) => this.lastElement() == null && (e != null) => this.lastElement().equals(e);

But need parentheses correct: (e == null) => this.lastElement() == null && (e != null) => this.lastElement().equals(e);

But precedence: => lower than &&, so we need parentheses: (e == null) => (this.lastElement() == null) && (e != null) => this.lastElement().equals(e);

Make sure to use this.lastElement() after addition (current state). That's fine.

Return value is always true, not needed.

Thus final assert:

<code>assert (e == null) => (this.lastElement() == null) && (e != null) => this.lastElement().equals(e);</code>

Check if need \old? Not needed because last element after addition is new element; we don't need old state.

